WN.lb(9,{"mt_mvXufozy2s":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Is it OK to ask for help?","choices":["Yes, after you have had a go","No, asking is babyish","Only if nobody is looking"],"answer":0,"hint":"Do grown-ups ever ask for help? When is it smart?","explain":"Everyone gets stuck. Asking after you have had a go is what good learners do."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Sam has been stuck on a sum for {n} minutes and feels cross. He has tried two ways. What now?","choices":["ask for help","keep sitting there","rip up the page"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the stuck meter in the lab. What happens if you sit stuck for a long time?","explain":"After {n} minutes and two tries, Sam should ask. Sitting stuck only makes him crosser.","params":{"n":[5,10]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Why ask for a clue, not the answer?","choices":["so you can finish it yourself and learn","because clues are quicker","so the helper does the work"],"answer":0,"hint":"Who does the learning if someone else gives the answer?","explain":"A clue lets you do the last bit yourself, so you learn it."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which of these needs a grown-up straight away?","choices":["someone is being unkind to you","you can't spell a word","your tower fell down"],"answer":0,"hint":"Hurt, unsafe or unkind problems are different from stuck-on-work problems.","explain":"If someone is unkind to you, tell a grown-up straight away. The others you can have a go at first."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"The teacher is busy reading with {n} children. You are stuck on a word but not hurt. What do you do?","choices":["try the word wall or a friend, then ask when the teacher is free","shout for the teacher","pull the teacher's arm"],"answer":0,"hint":"You are not hurt, so it can wait a little. What can you try first?","explain":"The teacher is busy with {n} children, so try the wall or a friend, then ask at a good moment.","params":{"n":[3,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"What does a good ask say?","choices":["what you are doing, what you tried and what you need","just Help!","I can't do it"],"answer":0,"hint":"Remember the three lines in Pip's speech bubble.","explain":"A good ask says what you are doing, what you tried and what you need, so the helper knows how to help."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Pip keeps trying the same way again and again. What should Pip do?","choices":["try a different way, or ask","keep going the same way","hide the work"],"answer":0,"hint":"What happened to Pip's stuck meter when Pip did the same thing again?","explain":"Doing the same thing again does not help. Try a different way, and if that fails, ask."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Leo asks for help before he even looks at the task. What might the helper say?","choices":["What have you tried?","Here is the answer.","Go away."],"answer":0,"hint":"What did the teacher say when Pip jumped straight to the top of the ladder?","explain":"The helper asks what Leo has tried, so he has a go first."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Ella has tried {n} different ways to spell a word and is still stuck. What should she do now?","choices":["ask a friend or a grown-up for a clue","keep trying the same way","give up"],"answer":0,"hint":"She has already had a go. What is the next rung on the help ladder?","explain":"Ella has tried {n} ways, so now asking for a clue is the smart move.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Before you ask for help with your work, what should you do first?","choices":["have a go and try another way","ask straight away","wait for someone to notice"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of Pip and the word 'they'. What fixed it before anyone helped?","explain":"Have a go first. Often a new try or a clue on the wall gets you unstuck."},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Max is doing a {n}-piece jigsaw and is stuck. Which ask is clearest?","choices":["I did the edges. Can you give me a clue for this corner?","I can't do it!","Do it for me."],"answer":0,"hint":"A good ask says what you did and what you need.","explain":"Saying what you did on the {n}-piece jigsaw and asking for a clue tells the helper exactly how to help.","params":{"n":[12,48]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Zara trips over and hurts her arm. What should she do?","choices":["tell a grown-up straight away","try to fix it herself first","say nothing"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is this a stuck-on-work problem, or a hurt or unsafe problem?","explain":"When you are hurt, get a grown-up straight away. You do not need to try first."}],"explain":{"kid":["Everyone gets stuck. Grown-ups get stuck too.","First, have a go. Try a new way. Look for a clue, like a word on the wall.","Still stuck? Ask a friend or a grown-up. Say what you tried. Ask for a clue, not the answer.","If you are hurt, or someone is unkind, get a grown-up right away. You do not need to try first."],"adult":["Knowing when and how to ask is a learning skill, not a weakness. Research on help-seeking separates two kinds. One is asking for a hint or an explanation so you can finish the job yourself, which goes with better learning. The other is asking someone to do it for you. Many UK classrooms teach a routine such as brain, book, buddy, boss: think, look for a clue, ask a friend, then ask the teacher.","For five- and six-year-olds the aim is a simple order: have a go, try a different way, look for a clue, then ask. Children this age also need a clear exception. If they are hurt, unsafe, unwell or someone is being unkind, they tell a grown-up straight away.","Common mistakes: asking before trying, so help becomes a habit and the child never builds stamina; the opposite, sitting stuck and upset because asking feels babyish; vague asks such as I can't do it, which leave the helper guessing; and asking for the answer instead of a clue. Adults slip too, by jumping in with the answer the moment a child hesitates.","At home, when your child is stuck, ask what they have tried before you help, then give the smallest clue that gets them going. Praise the ask itself: good thinking to tell me what you tried. Say out loud when you are stuck and how you get help."]},"familyTip":"Next time your child says \"I can't do it\", ask \"What have you tried?\" first. Then give one small clue, not the answer, and praise them for asking.","keyIdea":"When you are stuck, have a go first: try another way, then look for a clue. If you are still stuck, ask a friend or a grown-up, and say what you tried and what you need, such as a clue. If you are hurt, unsafe or someone is unkind, get a grown-up straight away. Asking after trying is what good learners do.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Does it ask for a clue, or for the answer? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Asks for a clue","Asks for the answer"],"items":[["Which sound does it start with?",0],["Can you show me the first step?",0],["Is there a picture to help me?",0],["Just tell me the word.",1],["Can you write it for me?",1],["What is the answer?",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flaghelp","caption":"Flagship lab: help Pip climb the help ladder, build a clear ask, and sort problems into now or try first.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_wvcFlwOrDl":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Red, red, green, red, red, green. How many shapes are in the part that repeats?","choices":["3","6","2"],"answer":0,"hint":"Point to where the row starts again. The part that repeats ends just before it.","explain":"Red, red, green is the part that repeats, so it has 3 shapes."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Ella says 2, 4, 8 comes next with 10. What did she do?","choices":["she used add 2 from the first jump only","she found the right rule","she doubled 8"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does add 2 work for 4 to 8?","explain":"Add 2 fits 2 to 4 but not 4 to 8. The rule that fits every jump is double, so 16 is next."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"A tower has {a} blocks, then {=a+3}, then {=a+6}. How many blocks come next?","answer":"a+9","hint":"How many blocks are added each time?","explain":"Three blocks are added each time, so the next tower has {=a+9} blocks.","params":{"a":[1,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Leo says, \"I runned home.\" What is going on?","choices":["he used the -ed pattern on a word that breaks it","runned is right","he forgot the pattern"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of jump, jumped. Does run follow that pattern?","explain":"Leo found the -ed pattern and used it. But run breaks it: we say ran."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday … Sunday, Monday. What kind of pattern is the week?","choices":["it repeats every 7 days","it grows","it has no pattern"],"answer":0,"hint":"What day comes after Sunday?","explain":"After Sunday the week starts again with Monday, so it repeats every 7 days."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"1, 2, 4, 7, 11. What is the rule?","choices":["add 1 more each time","double each time","add 2 each time"],"answer":0,"hint":"Write the jumps: 1 to 2, 2 to 4, 4 to 7 …","explain":"The jumps are +1, +2, +3, +4, so you add 1 more each time. Double would give 8 after 4."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Why is it good to say why a pattern works?","choices":["so you can check it and use it on new things","so it takes longer","you do not need a reason"],"answer":0,"hint":"If you know the rule, can you go on past the numbers you can see?","explain":"A reason lets you test the pattern and use it to predict things you have not seen yet."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"Clap, clap, stamp is the part that repeats. You do it {n} times. How many moves is that?","answer":"3*n","hint":"Each time through the part that repeats is 3 moves: clap, clap, stamp.","explain":"3 moves each time, {n} times, makes {=3*n} moves.","params":{"n":[2,6]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"Ben counts on in {d}s: {d}, {=2*d}, {=3*d}. What number comes next?","answer":"4*d","hint":"Find the jump between each pair of numbers, then make the same jump again.","explain":"Each jump is {d}, so the next number is {=3*d} + {d} = {=4*d}.","params":{"d":[2,10]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Star, moon, moon, star, moon, moon, star. What comes next?","choices":["moon","star","sun"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find the part that repeats first. Then carry it on.","explain":"The part that repeats is star, moon, moon. After star comes moon."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A pattern doubles each time: {n}, {=2*n}, {=4*n}. What comes next?","answer":"8*n","hint":"Doubling means you add the number to itself.","explain":"Double {=4*n} is {=8*n}. Check: {n} doubled is {=2*n}, and {=2*n} doubled is {=4*n}.","params":{"n":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Sam sees 5, 10 and says the next must be 15. Is he sure?","choices":["not yet: 5, 10 could add 5 or double","yes, it adds 5","yes, it doubles"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can you think of two rules that both turn 5 into 10?","explain":"Add 5 gives 15 and double gives 20. Two numbers fit both rules, so he needs one more to be sure."}],"explain":{"kid":["A pattern is a thing that happens again and again. Red, blue, blue, red, blue, blue. The part that repeats is red, blue, blue.","Some patterns grow. 2, 4, 8, 16. Each number is double the one before. That is the rule.","Test the rule on every jump, not just the first one. Add 2 works for 2 to 4. But 4 add 2 is 6, not 8. So add 2 is wrong. If two rules fit, look at one more. Then you can be sure.","Words have patterns too. Jump, jumped. Play, played. But we say ran, not runned. So check that it sounds right."],"adult":["Spotting patterns means noticing structure that repeats or changes by a rule, and then using it to predict. Children meet it in repeating shape and colour sequences, counting in steps, times tables, word families and spelling rules, and in daily events such as the order of the week. The UK national curriculum asks for sequences and counting in steps from Year 1, and the habit underpins later algebra.","A strong pattern-spotter does three things: finds the unit of repeat or the rule, checks it against every term, and explains why it works. Research on early pattern work suggests that naming the unit of repeat, rather than just saying the next item, is what builds later reasoning.","Common mistakes: copying the last item instead of finding the unit of repeat; choosing a rule from the first two terms only, so 2, 4, 8 becomes 10; being certain too early, when a short run such as 1, 2, 4 fits more than one rule; and overgeneralising a language pattern, as in goed or runned. That last slip is a good sign: it shows the child has found the rule and is using it.","At home, make patterns with cutlery, claps or Lego and ask your child what the part that repeats is. Ask how they know what comes next, and ask them to test the rule on every step. Now and then, make a pattern with a twist and see if they spot it."]},"familyTip":"Clap a pattern such as clap, clap, tap, and ask your child to carry it on and say the part that repeats. Then let them make one for you.","keyIdea":"A pattern is something that repeats or grows by a rule. Find the part that repeats, or the rule, and you can say what comes next. Test a rule on every step, not just the first one, and look for more examples before you are sure. Some patterns break, like run and ran, so check that your guess makes sense.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Does it repeat, or does it grow? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Repeats","Grows"],"items":[["red, blue, red, blue",0],["2, 4, 6, 8",1],["tick, tock, tick, tock",0],["1, 2, 4, 8",1],["Mon, Tue, Wed … Sun, Mon",0],["towers of 1, 2, 3 blocks",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagpat","caption":"Flagship lab: find the part that repeats, test number rules on every jump, try a word pattern, and build your own.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_S4G6GLKr1-":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"You get a word wrong in your spelling. What does the mistake tell you?","choices":["which part to fix","that you are bad at spelling","that you should hide your work"],"answer":0,"hint":"A mistake is a clue. What is it a clue about?","explain":"A mistake shows you which part to fix. Everyone makes mistakes when they learn."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Ben has tried a puzzle the same way {n} times and it still does not work. What should he do?","choices":["try a different way","do the same thing again","throw the puzzle away"],"answer":0,"hint":"What happened in the lab when Pip threw with the same power twice?","explain":"Ben tried the same way {n} times. The same try gives the same result, so he should change something.","params":{"n":[3,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which of these is a break, not giving up?","choices":["I'll have a drink and come back to it.","I'm never doing this again.","I'll say I finished when I didn't."],"answer":0,"hint":"A break has a plan to come back.","explain":"Having a drink and coming back is a break. The other two stop the learning."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Your friend can already tie her laces. You can't yet. What is true?","choices":["people learn things at different speeds","you will never tie laces","your friend is cleverer at everything"],"answer":0,"hint":"Did Pip ride the bike on day 1?","explain":"People learn at different speeds. With practice you will get there too."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Ava wants to hop on one foot for {n} hops. Today she can only do 2. What will help most?","choices":["practise a little every day","give up: some kids just can't hop","try once and stop"],"answer":0,"hint":"How did the wobbles change when Pip practised each day?","explain":"Practising a little every day will help Ava get closer to {n} hops.","params":{"n":[5,10]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which word turns \"I can't read this\" into a keep-going thought?","choices":["yet","never","ever"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which word means you are still learning it?","explain":"\"I can't read this yet\" means you are learning and will get there."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Sam tried three different ways and is still stuck after a long time. What now?","choices":["ask for a clue, then keep going","ask someone to do it for him","keep trying the same way"],"answer":0,"hint":"Keeping going does not mean doing it all alone. What kind of help still lets Sam learn?","explain":"After trying different ways, a clue helps Sam keep going and finish it himself."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"What does it mean when something feels hard?","choices":["you are learning something new","you are not smart","you should stop"],"answer":0,"hint":"Was riding a bike hard for Pip at first? Is it still hard after practice?","explain":"Hard means you are learning something new. It gets easier with practice."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Mo throws a ball at a target. It lands {n} steps too short. What should Mo try next?","choices":["throw a bit harder","throw the same way again","stop and say he is bad at throwing"],"answer":0,"hint":"Too short is a clue. What change would make the ball go further?","explain":"The ball fell {n} steps short, so the clue says throw a bit harder. The same throw would land in the same place.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Which thought helps you keep trying?","choices":["I can't do it yet.","I'm no good at this.","It is too hard for me."],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for the little word that means you will get there.","explain":"The word yet means you are still learning. The other two make you want to stop."},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Lily practised her bike for {n} days. On day 1 she fell a lot. What most likely happened?","choices":["she got steadier as she practised","she fell just as much every day","she forgot how to ride"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of Pip's wobbles in the lab. What happened to them each day of practice?","explain":"With {n} days of practice, Lily got steadier. Practice makes hard things easier.","params":{"n":[4,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Kit's block tower fell down three times. Kit feels very cross. What is the best thing to do?","choices":["take a calm break, then try a new way","kick the blocks","never build again"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is a short break the same as giving up?","explain":"A calm break helps Kit think again. Then a new way, like big blocks at the bottom, can work."}],"explain":{"kid":["Some things are hard. That is OK. Hard means you are learning.","A mistake is a clue. If your throw is too short, throw a bit harder next time. Try a new way. The same try gives the same result.","Say \"I can't do it yet.\" The word yet means you will get there.","Feel cross? Take a break. Breathe in and out. Then try again."],"adult":["Persistence at this age means staying with a tricky task, treating a mistake as information and trying a different approach rather than repeating the same move or giving up. The English early years framework lists it as a characteristic of effective learning: keeping on trying when challenges occur and bouncing back after difficulties.","Two ideas help five- and six-year-olds most. First, a mistake tells you what to change: too short means harder, a tower that falls means a different order. Second, skills grow with practice, so today's wobble is not a fixed trait. The word yet captures this: I can't ride my bike yet.","Common mistakes: praising persistence that is really repetition, when a child tries the same thing ten times and gets more upset; treating a break as quitting, when a short calm break is often what lets a child return; praising being clever, which can make children avoid hard tasks so they keep the label; and comparing with a sibling or friend who learned faster. Research on mindset messages shows small average effects, so the daily habits matter more than slogans.","At home, choose something just a little too hard, such as a jigsaw, a skipping rope or a tricky word. When your child makes a mistake, ask what it tells them to change. Notice effort and new strategies out loud: you tried a different way that time. Let them see you get something wrong and try again."]},"familyTip":"When your child says \"I can't do it\", add \"yet\" together, then ask \"What could you try differently?\" Praise the new try, not just the result.","keyIdea":"Hard things get easier when you keep trying. A mistake is a clue that shows what to change, so try a different way instead of the same way again. Say \"I can't do it yet\", take a calm break if you feel cross, then have another go.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is it a break, or giving up? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["A break, then try again","Giving up"],"items":[["I'll have a drink and come back.",0],["I'll take three deep breaths.",0],["I'll rest, then try a new way.",0],["I'm never doing this again.",1],["I'll put it in the bin.",1],["I'll say I'm done when I'm not.",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagtry","caption":"Flagship lab: use each miss as a clue, practise day by day, pick keep-going thoughts, and take a break before trying a new way.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_hbe_kdE_7C":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which is a rule, not just one example?","choices":["Any number times 1 stays the same","6 × 1 = 6","Times is fun"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for one that talks about every number.","explain":"It covers any number, says what you do (times 1) and what happens (stays the same)."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Claim: all birds can fly. Which is a breaker?","choices":["A penguin","A robin","A pigeon"],"answer":0,"hint":"A breaker is a case where the claim goes wrong.","explain":"A penguin is a bird that cannot fly, so it breaks the claim. Robins and pigeons fit it."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"Rule: odd + odd = even. Test a new case. What is {a} + {b}?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Add them, then look at the last digit.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b}. It ends in an even digit, so the rule held for this new case.","params":{"a":[11,41],"b":[13,59]},"where":"a%2==1 && b%2==1"},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Rule: adding makes a number bigger. Which case breaks it?","choices":["7 + 0","7 + 2","7 + 10"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which sum gives back the same number?","explain":"7 + 0 = 7, which is not bigger. A better rule: adding a number more than 0 makes it bigger."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Ben tested odd + odd 50 times. All were even. Has he proved it for every number?","choices":["No, he needs a reason why it must work","Yes, 50 is plenty","Yes, if a friend agrees"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can he test every odd number there is?","explain":"50 examples help him notice. The reason (two spare dots make a pair) shows it works for all odd numbers."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"Rule: any number ending in 0 can be split into two equal whole halves. Test a new case: what is half of {n}0?","answer":"n*5","hint":"Halve the tens, then check the two halves are equal whole numbers.","explain":"Half of {n}0 is {=n*5}, a whole number, so the rule held for this new case.","params":{"n":[3,99]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Why is odd + odd always even?","choices":["The two spare dots make a pair","Because 3 + 5 = 8","Odd numbers are big"],"answer":0,"hint":"Draw an odd number as pairs of dots. What is left over?","explain":"Each odd number is pairs plus one. The two spare ones join to make a new pair, so nothing is left over."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which is the better fixed rule for times?","choices":["Times a number above 0 by more than 1 and it gets bigger","Times always makes it bigger","Times never makes it bigger"],"answer":0,"hint":"Check 6 × 1, 6 × 0 and 0 × 5.","explain":"6 × 1, 6 × 0 and 0 × 5 broke the old claim. The fixed rule leaves them out, and 6 × 3 still gets bigger."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"4 + 0 = 4, 9 + 0 = 9, 20 + 0 = 20. Which is the best rule?","choices":["Any number add 0 stays the same","4 + 0 = 4","Adding is easy"],"answer":0,"hint":"A rule should cover every number, not just one example.","explain":"It says which numbers (any), what you do (add 0) and what happens (stays the same)."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Rule: even + even = even. Which new case tests the rule? {a} + {b}, or 2 + 4 again?","choices":["{a} + {b}","2 + 4 again","Neither, it is already proved"],"answer":0,"hint":"A good test uses a case you have not tried yet.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b}, which is even, so the rule holds on a new case. Testing 2 + 4 again tells you nothing new.","params":{"a":[4,20],"b":[100,120]},"where":"a%2==0 && b%2==0"},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"Claim: multiplying always makes a number bigger. What is {n} × 1?","answer":"n","hint":"What happens when you have {n} one time?","explain":"{n} × 1 = {=n}. It did not get bigger, so this case breaks the claim.","params":{"n":[3,30]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"A rule worked for 10 examples, then broke once. What now?","choices":["Fix the rule so it fits the breaker too","Ignore the breaker, 10 is more","Say the rule is proved"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does one case that fails matter?","explain":"One breaker shows the rule is not always true. Add a condition so the rule fits every case."}],"explain":{"kid":["3 + 0 = 3. 7 + 0 = 7. 12 + 0 = 12. You can say a rule: any number add 0 stays the same. A rule says what you do and what happens. It says it for every number, not just one.","Next, test your rule on new cases. Try one you have not seen yet. Try a big one, like 1000 + 0. If it still works, you can trust the rule a bit more.","Now hunt for a breaker. That is a case where the rule goes wrong. Say your rule is: times makes a number bigger. But 6 × 1 = 6. That breaks it. One breaker is enough.","Then fix the rule. Times a number by more than 1, and it gets bigger. To be sure it always works, find a reason. Odd numbers are pairs plus one dot. Two odd numbers have two spare dots. They make a pair, so odd + odd is even."],"adult":["This is the move from noticing to generalising. Children often state a single example (5 + 0 = 5) or a vague feeling (adding zero is easy) and call it a rule. Help them say it in three parts: which numbers or words it covers, what you do, and what happens.","Testing comes next. Ask for a case they have not seen, especially an extreme one: a very big number, zero, or a word that looks different. A rule that only fits the examples it came from is not yet trustworthy.","The key idea is the counterexample. Twenty cases that fit cannot show a rule is always true, but one case that fails shows it is not. The usual response is not to throw the rule away but to add a condition, as with multiplying a positive number by more than one.","A common mistake is to treat many examples as proof. Ask why it must work. For odd plus odd, every odd number is pairs plus one, and the two spare ones make a new pair. That reason covers every odd number, which no list of examples can do."]},"familyTip":"Play 'rule or breaker' at dinner. One person says a rule, like 'all fruits are sweet' or 'every number ending in 0 can be halved'. Everyone else hunts for a breaker. If you find one, work together to fix the rule.","keyIdea":"When you notice something happening again and again, say it as a rule that works every time: what you start with, what you do, and what happens. Then test the rule on new cases you have not seen, and hunt for a breaker. One breaker shows the rule is not always true, so fix it. Lots of examples help you notice a rule, but only a reason shows it must always work.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is it a rule or just one example? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["A rule","One example"],"items":[["Any number add 0 stays the same",0],["8 + 0 = 8",1],["Even + even is even",0],["2 + 4 = 6",1],["Any number times 1 stays the same",0],["5 × 1 = 5",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagrule","caption":"Flagship lab: build a rule from examples, test new cases, hunt for a breaker, fix the rule and prove odd + odd is even.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_8dstvf-KKb":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"How should you count again?","choices":["Touch each thing one time","Look fast and guess","Touch some things two times"],"answer":0,"hint":"How did Pip count the apples?","explain":"Touching each thing once means you skip none and count none twice."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"\"How many legs has a dog?\" Leo says, \"A dog has a tail.\" Does it answer the question?","choices":["No, it is about a tail, not legs","Yes, it is true","Yes, dogs are nice"],"answer":0,"hint":"What did the question ask about?","explain":"It is true, but the question was about legs. A dog has 4 legs."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Read it back: \"Iwent home.\" What is the slip?","choices":["I and went need a space","It needs a capital","Nothing is wrong"],"answer":0,"hint":"Are the words too close together?","explain":"I and went are two words, so they need a finger space between them."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"What is the best order?","choices":["Finish, look back, fix, say done","Say done, then finish","Finish, say done, never look"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the lab. Which step comes last of all?","explain":"You finish, look back, fix any slips, and say done last of all."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Zak says {n} + 2 is {=n+3}. How can he check?","choices":["Make it with counters","Say it louder","Ask him to stop"],"answer":0,"hint":"How did Pip check sums in the lab?","explain":"Counters show {n} and 2 more is {=n+2}, so Zak can fix {=n+3}.","params":{"n":[2,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Ava counts {n} ducks, touching each one once. She wrote {n}. What now?","choices":["It matches, so she can say done","Change it to a bigger number","Count with her eyes shut"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does her count match what she wrote?","explain":"Her careful count and her answer are both {n}, so the check says it is right.","params":{"n":[3,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Gran asks, \"What did you check?\" What is a good answer?","choices":["I read each line back for full stops","I don't know","I just said done"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can you say the check you did?","explain":"Saying what you checked, like reading back for full stops, shows you really looked."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Sam checks his sum the same way twice and gets the same slip. What helps?","choices":["Check another way, like with counters","Check the same way again","Stop checking"],"answer":0,"hint":"What did the ten frame do in the lab?","explain":"A new way, like counters, can show a slip that the first way keeps missing."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Sam has just finished his work. What should he do first?","choices":["Look back at it","Say done straight away","Throw it away"],"answer":0,"hint":"What comes between finish and done?","explain":"Look back first. Then fix any slips, and only then say done."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Pip wrote {n} apples. Pip counts again and there are {=n+1}. What should Pip do?","choices":["Fix it to {=n+1}","Keep {n}","Rub it all out"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which number did the careful count find?","explain":"The careful count found {=n+1}, so Pip changes {n} to {=n+1}.","params":{"n":[3,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Read it back: \"the cat sat.\" What needs fixing?","choices":["A capital T at the start","A space in cat","Nothing at all"],"answer":0,"hint":"How should a sentence start?","explain":"A sentence starts with a capital letter, so the needs a big T."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Mia says {n} + 1 is {=n+2}. She checks with counters. What will she find?","choices":["It is {=n+1}, so she fixes it","She was right","Counters can not help"],"answer":0,"hint":"Put out {n} counters, then add one more. How many?","explain":"{n} counters and 1 more makes {=n+1}, not {=n+2}. Checking another way found the slip.","params":{"n":[2,7]}}],"explain":{"kid":["When you finish, do not stop yet. Look back at your work. Ask: does this look right? Then you can say I am done.","Count again. Touch each thing one time. Pip wrote 4 apples, but there are 5. Pip fixed it.","Read your words back. Is there a capital at the start? A space between words? A full stop at the end?","Ask: does my answer fit the question? Try a sum a new way, with counters. Then say what you checked."],"adult":["This topic is self-checking. A child looks back over finished work without being asked, finds at least one error, and can say what they checked. It is an early step in metacognition: children learn to watch their own thinking instead of waiting for an adult to mark it.","The lab gives each kind of check its own action. Children recount apples, touching each one once. They read a sentence back and fix a capital letter, a finger space and a full stop. They decide if answers fit their questions, check sums with counters on a ten frame, and put the look-back steps in order.","Common mistakes: saying done as soon as the last mark is made; recounting by glancing instead of touching, so an item is skipped or counted twice; reading what they meant to write, not what is on the page; giving a true answer to a different question; and checking the same way twice, which repeats the same slip.","Build a short routine at home: finish, look back, fix, then say done. Ask what did you check? Praise the checking itself, most of all when your child finds their own slip."]},"familyTip":"After a drawing, puzzle or bit of writing, ask \"What will you check before you say done?\" Let your child find one thing to fix by themselves, then ask them to tell you what they checked.","keyIdea":"When you finish, look back before you say done. Count again, touching each thing once. Read your writing back. Ask: does my answer fit the question? Check a sum another way. Fix any slips, then say what you checked.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is it a way to check your work? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Checks the work","Skips the check"],"items":[["Count again",0],["Read it back",0],["Try it with counters",0],["Say done straight away",1],["Hand it in, no look",1],["Guess and stop",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagcheck","caption":"Flagship lab: recount Pip's apples one touch each, read a sentence back to fix it, test if answers fit, check sums with counters and order the look-back steps.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_6eTZUwKQZr":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What does teaching it back mean?","choices":["Explaining what you learned in your own words","Reading the page again","Copying the teacher's words"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of Teddy. What did you do for him?","explain":"Teaching it back means explaining it yourself, to a person or a toy, in your own words."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Who can you teach it back to?","choices":["A grown-up, a friend or even a toy","Only a teacher","Only someone older than you"],"answer":0,"hint":"Pip taught Teddy. Does the listener have to be a person?","explain":"Anyone works, even a toy. Saying it out loud is what helps you learn."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Mia gets stuck when her brother asks why. What does that show?","choices":["A gap she can learn next","That she is bad at learning","That the question was silly"],"answer":0,"hint":"What did Pip do after getting stuck?","explain":"Getting stuck shows a gap. Now Mia knows exactly what to find out."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Idea: magnets pull iron. Which example fits?","choices":["An iron nail","A plastic ruler","Kitchen foil"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one is made of iron? Not every metal is iron.","explain":"An iron nail sticks. Plastic does not, and foil is a metal that is not iron, so it does not stick either."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Leo says the teacher's exact words {n} times. Has he explained it?","choices":["No, a strong explanation needs the idea, his own example and why","Yes, saying it more makes it an explanation","Yes, if he says it loudly"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the three boxes about melting ice. Was any of that copied?","explain":"Saying it {n} times is still copying. A strong explanation has the idea, your own example and why it happens.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Raj says, \"My teacher told me so.\" Is that an explanation?","choices":["No, it says who told him, not why","Yes, teachers are always right","Yes, it is the best kind"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does it say why the idea is true?","explain":"It names who told him but gives no reason. An explanation says why."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"You learned {n} things about the Moon. You can only teach three. What should you pick?","choices":["The three ideas that matter most","The first three you heard","The funniest three"],"answer":0,"hint":"What did you put on Teddy's card about bees?","explain":"Out of {n}, pick the key ideas that help someone understand the Moon, not just any three.","params":{"n":[6,12]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Why does teaching it back help you learn?","choices":["Explaining shows what you know and what you do not","It is quicker than learning","It means you never forget anything"],"answer":0,"hint":"What did Pip find out while teaching Teddy?","explain":"Putting it in your own words checks your understanding and shows the gaps. It does not make you remember everything forever."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"The teacher said, \"Sound travels as vibrations.\" Which shows Ana really understands?","choices":["When I hit a drum it shakes, and the shaking moves through the air to my ears","Sound travels as vibrations","Sound is loud"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one uses her own words and her own example?","explain":"Ana explains it her own way, with a drum example. Repeating the exact words could just be copying."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Leo teaches Teddy about the {n} times table. Teddy asks why, and Leo is stuck. What should Leo do?","choices":["Find out, then teach it again","Make something up","Stop teaching"],"answer":0,"hint":"Getting stuck shows a gap. What fills a gap?","explain":"Being stuck on the {n} times table shows Leo what to learn next. He finds out, then teaches it again.","params":{"n":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Idea: some things dissolve in water. Which is an example you made up yourself?","choices":["Sugar in my tea disappears when I stir it","The salt the teacher showed us","Sand in a bucket of water"],"answer":0,"hint":"It should be your own, and it must fit the idea.","explain":"Sugar in tea is your own example and it dissolves. Salt was the teacher's, and sand does not dissolve."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"You learned {n} facts about frogs. You can teach Teddy three. Which is a key idea?","choices":["Frogs start life as tadpoles in water","The book had a green cover","My friend likes frogs"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one helps Teddy understand frogs?","explain":"Out of {n} facts, frogs starting as tadpoles is a key idea. The book cover and your friend are small details.","params":{"n":[5,10]}}],"explain":{"kid":["You just learned something new. Now teach it to someone else. A grown-up, a friend or a teddy will do.","Use your own words. Do not just copy what the teacher said. Copying can sound right, even when you do not get it.","A good way to teach has three parts. Say the idea. Give an example you thought of. Then say why it happens.","Did you get stuck when Teddy asked why? Good! That is a gap. Now you know what to find out. Teach it again once you know."],"adult":["Teaching it back means explaining something new in your own words to someone else, or to a toy. Some call it learning by teaching. Studies of self-explanation and of learning by teaching show that putting ideas into words, and then answering questions, builds memory and understanding more than reading it again does.","The lab practises each part. Children choose the explanation that teaches Teddy rather than a word-for-word copy or a wrong idea, build an explanation from an idea, their own example and a reason, find where Pip gets stuck when Teddy asks why, test their own examples on a magnet, and choose the three key ideas from a lesson on bees.","Common mistakes come up often. Children repeat the teacher's exact words, which can sound right with no understanding behind it. They reuse the teacher's example instead of making one up. They give an example that does not fit, such as thinking a magnet pulls all metals. They see getting stuck as failure, when it shows what to learn next. And they fill a summary with small details instead of key ideas.","After school, ask your child to teach you one thing they learned, as if you knew nothing. Ask why, and then ask for an example of their own. If they get stuck, say, great, that is what we can find out, and look it up together."]},"familyTip":"Once a week, let your child be the teacher. They teach you, or a toy, one thing from school for two minutes. Ask one why question and one for an example of their own, and praise them when they spot something they do not know yet.","keyIdea":"After you learn something, teach it back in your own words, to a person or even a toy. Say the idea, give an example you made up, and say why. Where you get stuck is a gap: now you know what to find out next.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is this teaching it back? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Teaching it back","Not teaching it back"],"items":[["Explain it to a toy",0],["Give your own example",0],["Say why it happens",0],["Copy the exact words",1],["Skip the hard part",1],["Make up an answer",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagteach","caption":"Flagship lab: teach Teddy in your own words, build an explanation, find the gap when Teddy asks why, test your own examples on a magnet and pick the key ideas.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_uClzF_I1Sv":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Story time. A toy beeps. What helps you hear the end?","choices":["Look back at the book","Play with the toy","Shut the book"],"answer":0,"hint":"What will tell you how the story ends?","explain":"Looking back at the book lets you hear the end. The toy pulls you away."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Pip plays with the puzzle, the drum and the ball all at once. Does Pip finish?","choices":["No. One thing at a time","Yes, all of them","Yes, it is faster"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can you finish lots of things if you keep jumping?","explain":"Doing it all at once means nothing gets finished. One thing at a time works."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Too many toys are out. What can help?","choices":["Put some away","Get more toys","Turn the TV up"],"answer":0,"hint":"Fewer things means fewer pulls on your eyes.","explain":"Putting some toys away leaves one thing to focus on."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"You looked away. Is that bad?","choices":["No, I can look back","Yes, now it is over","Yes, I am naughty"],"answer":0,"hint":"What did we do after the dog barked?","explain":"Everyone looks away sometimes. The trick is to look back and carry on."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"You put {n} toys away. Now only the puzzle is left. Is it easier?","choices":["Yes, nothing pulls me away","No, it is harder","No, I need the TV"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count what is left to look at.","explain":"With {n} toys put away, only the puzzle is left, so it is easier to stay with it.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"You feel wiggly at story time. What can help?","choices":["A big breath, then listen","Run off","Talk over the story"],"answer":0,"hint":"What helps your body feel calm?","explain":"A big breath can help you feel calm so you can keep listening."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Find the duck! There are {n} shiny things too. What do you do?","choices":["Keep looking for the duck","Tap the shiny things","Give up"],"answer":0,"hint":"What is the one thing you are looking for?","explain":"Keep looking for the duck. The {n} shiny things are there to pull your eyes away.","params":{"n":[3,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"You got lost in the game. Who can help you come back?","choices":["A grown-up","Nobody","The TV"],"answer":0,"hint":"Who is nearby and can help you find your place?","explain":"A grown-up can help you find your place and come back to the game."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"You are doing a puzzle. A dog barks and you look. What now?","choices":["Look back at the puzzle","Stop the puzzle for ever","Get a new toy"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is the puzzle over just because you looked away?","explain":"Looking away is OK. Look back at the puzzle and keep going."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Your tower has {n} cups. A bird flies by. What helps the tower grow?","choices":["Look back at the cups","Watch the bird","Knock it down"],"answer":0,"hint":"The tower only grows when your eyes are on what?","explain":"Looking back at the cups lets you add more to your {n} cups. Watching the bird stops the tower.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Where is it easier to do a puzzle?","choices":["With just the puzzle out","With the TV on loud","With lots of toys out"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which room has fewer things pulling your eyes away?","explain":"Just the puzzle is easiest. Loud TV and lots of toys pull your eyes away."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"A story has {n} pages. You stop on page 2. Do you know the end?","choices":["No, the end is on the last page","Yes, I know it all","Yes, page 2 is the end"],"answer":0,"hint":"Where in a book does the story finish?","explain":"The end is on the last page of the {n}, so stopping on page 2 means you miss it.","params":{"n":[4,8]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Pick one thing, like a book or a puzzle. Look at it. Stay with it a little while.","A bird flies by. A dog barks. Your eyes look away. That is OK! Look back and keep going.","If you stop, you miss the end. Kit the cat looked and looked. Only the last page said where her ball was.","Too many toys make it hard. Put some away. Turn the TV off. Then just one thing is left."],"adult":["This topic is the start of focused attention: staying with one activity, such as a story, a puzzle or stacking cups, for a few minutes, and coming back to it after a distraction, often with an adult's help. Two- and three-year-olds are easily pulled away; the skill is returning, not never looking up.","The lab gives each part its own action. Children stack cups while a bird tempts them to look, hear a short story to the end despite a beeping TV, return to a fish puzzle after a dog barks, put noisy things away so only the puzzle is left, and keep finding one duck among shiny pictures.","Common mistakes: expecting a toddler to sit still for a long time; adults interrupting a child who is already focused; leaving the TV on in the background, which studies link to shorter, more broken toy play; putting out lots of toys at once, when fewer toys lead to longer play with each; and treating one look away as the end of the activity.","To help, notice what holds your child's interest and join in. When they drift, name the activity and invite them back: \"Where were we? The red cup is next.\" Praise the coming back, not just the finishing."]},"familyTip":"Choose one short picture book and read it in a quiet spot with the TV off. If your child wanders, pause, wait, then say, \"Let's see what happens next,\" and carry on. Do this most days and notice them staying a little longer.","keyIdea":"Pick one thing and stay with it for a little while. Things will try to pull your eyes away. That is OK. Look back and keep going. A calm spot with just one thing out makes it easier.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Does it help you stay with one thing? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Helps me stay","Pulls me away"],"items":[["TV off",0],["One toy out",0],["A quiet spot",0],["Look back after a noise",0],["TV on loud",1],["Lots of toys out",1],["A buzzing phone",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagfocus","caption":"Flagship lab: stack cups past a bird, hear a story to the end, come back to a puzzle, clear noisy things and keep your eyes on the duck.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_3ocYHHKvdm":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What is the main difference between working and long-term memory?","choices":["Working memory is small and short; long-term memory is huge and lasting","They are the same thing with two names","Long-term memory is small; working memory is huge"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the small desk and the huge store in the lab.","explain":"Working memory is a small desk for what you are thinking about now. Long-term memory stores facts and skills for years."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"A door code has {n} digits. What helps you hold it in working memory?","choices":["Group the digits into a few chunks","Say all the digits faster","Look at it once and hope"],"answer":0,"hint":"How did FBIBBCUSA fit on the desk?","explain":"Grouping {n} digits into a few chunks means fewer slots. That is why phone numbers are often written in groups.","params":{"n":[8,12]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"What does the forgetting curve show?","choices":["We forget fast at first, then more slowly","We forget at the same speed every day","We never forget what we have learned"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the shape of the grey line in the lab.","explain":"The curve drops steeply soon after learning and then flattens. Forgetting is normal, and reviews slow it down."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Why does re-reading feel useful but often is not?","choices":["The page gets familiar, which feels like knowing, but it builds little memory","Re-reading always works best","Re-reading makes the words harder to read"],"answer":0,"hint":"In the study, which group felt most sure, and which remembered most?","explain":"Re-reading makes the page look familiar, so it feels known. But recall builds a stronger memory, as the one-week results showed."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Your test is in {n} days. Which plan will help you remember most?","choices":["Short recall quizzes on a few spread-out days","Re-read everything the night before","Read it all several times today"],"answer":0,"hint":"Spread out or all at once? Re-read or recall?","explain":"With {n} days, spread out a few recall sessions. Each one slows the forgetting curve. Cramming fades fast.","params":{"n":[5,14]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"In the 2006 study, who remembered the most after one week?","choices":["Students who read once and then recalled three times","Students who read the text four times","Both groups remembered the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the one-week bars: 40%, 56% and 61%.","explain":"The recall group kept 61% after a week. The re-read group kept 40%, even though it did best after five minutes."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"You learn the word 'herbivore'. Which link will help you find it later?","choices":["Herbs are plants, and a herbivore eats plants","I learned it on a Tuesday","My pencil was blue that day"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which link has a real connection to what the word means?","explain":"Linking herbivore to herbs, which are plants, gives a real path back to the meaning. A random detail gives no useful cue."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"You forgot most of a lesson by next week. What is true?","choices":["Forgetting is normal; recalling it now will rebuild it","It means you are bad at the subject","The lesson is gone and cannot come back"],"answer":0,"hint":"What did each review do to the curve in the lab?","explain":"Everyone forgets. Recalling it now makes the memory stronger, and the next drop will be slower."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Your friend reads out a list of {n} random words, once. Why can you only repeat a few back?","choices":["Working memory holds only about four things at once","Long-term memory is already full","You were not trying hard enough"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the desk in the lab. How many slots did it have?","explain":"Working memory is a small desk of about four slots. With {n} random words, new ones push the old ones off. Long-term memory has room to spare.","params":{"n":[8,15]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Which string is easiest to hold in mind?","choices":["CAT DOG SUN","XQZ RPT VLK","Both are equally easy"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which letters make chunks that long-term memory already knows?","explain":"CAT, DOG and SUN are words you know, so each is one chunk: three slots. The random letters need nine slots, which will not fit."},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"You learn {n} new French words on Monday. When will you forget the most, the fastest?","choices":["In the first day or so","Evenly, a little each day","Only after a month"],"answer":0,"hint":"Picture the forgetting curve. Where is it steepest?","explain":"The forgetting curve drops steeply at first. Many of the {n} words will slip within a day unless you recall them soon.","params":{"n":[10,20]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Sam re-reads his notes and thinks, this all looks familiar, I know it. What is the problem?","choices":["Familiar is not the same as being able to recall it","Nothing, familiar means he knows it","He should read it much faster"],"answer":0,"hint":"With the notes in front of him, is he ever asked to pull anything out of his head?","explain":"The notes feel familiar because they are in front of him. Only shutting them and recalling shows what he can really remember."}],"explain":{"kid":["Your memory has two main parts. Working memory is what you hold in mind right now. It is like a small desk. It holds only about four things at once. Add a fifth, and something falls off.","Long-term memory is a huge store. It holds facts, words and skills for years. What you already know helps the desk. FBI, BBC and USA are nine letters, but they take just three slots because you know them. That trick is called chunking.","Forgetting is normal. After a lesson, you forget fast at first, and then more slowly. This is the forgetting curve. Each time you pull the idea back out of your head, the next drop is slower. So review soon, then wait longer each time.","Re-reading feels useful because the page looks familiar. But familiar is not the same as known. In one study, students who recalled a text remembered 61% a week later. Students who only re-read it remembered 40%. Shut the book and test yourself. Link new ideas to ones you know, too. Each link is a path back."],"adult":["This topic gives learners a simple working model of memory. Working memory holds a few items at a time. Cowan's review puts it at about four chunks. Long-term memory has no known limit, and what is stored there lets several items be held as one chunk. Forgetting is normal. Ebbinghaus showed that it is steep at first and then levels off. Recall and links to prior knowledge slow it down.","The lab makes each idea something to do. Learners overload a four-slot desk and see old items pushed off. They chunk FBIBBCUSA into three known pieces. They place up to three reviews on a model forgetting curve, clearly labelled as a model, to keep a memory above a line all week. They reveal the results of Roediger and Karpicke's 2006 study. Last, they link a new word to ideas that truly connect.","The most common mistake is thinking that re-reading and highlighting are the best ways to study. They feel good because the text gets familiar, so learners feel sure they know it. In the 2006 study, the re-read group felt most confident but remembered least a week later. Other mistakes are cramming the night before, reading forgetting as a sign of being bad at a subject, and thinking memory is a fixed recording that just plays back.","To help at home, ask your learner to shut the book and tell you or write down what they remember. Then check it against the book. Before a test, plan three or four short recall sessions on spread-out days, not one long re-read the night before."]},"familyTip":"After homework, ask: what are three things you learned today, with the book shut? Two days later, ask again. Then ask again at the weekend. Notice together how much comes back each time.","keyIdea":"Working memory is a small desk that holds about four things at once. Long-term memory is a huge store. Forgetting is normal and fastest at first. Memories last longer when you recall them on spread-out days and link them to what you already know. Re-reading feels useful because the page looks familiar, but it builds less memory than recall.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which study habits build lasting memory? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Builds memory","Feels useful, builds little"],"items":[["Quiz yourself, book shut",0],["Review on spread-out days",0],["Link it to what you know",0],["Re-read the notes again",1],["Highlight whole pages",1],["Cram the night before",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagmem","caption":"Flagship lab: overload a small memory desk, chunk letters, space reviews on a forgetting curve, compare re-reading with recall and link a new word to cues.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt__GDhdZF948":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What does evaluate ask you to do?","choices":["Weigh both sides, then make a judgement with a reason","Describe what happens in detail","List as many facts as you can"],"answer":0,"hint":"How does an evaluate answer end?","explain":"Evaluate means weigh evidence for and against, then decide and say why. A description alone has no judgement."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"What does explain ask you to do?","choices":["Give reasons why something happens","Say what something looks like","Write about only one side"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which small words show a reason?","explain":"Explain asks for reasons: because, so, which means. Saying what something looks like is describe."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"A paper has {m} marks and lasts {=m+5} minutes. You keep 5 minutes to check. How many minutes should a {q}-mark question get?","answer":"q","hint":"Take off the checking time, then divide the minutes by the marks.","explain":"{=m+5} − 5 = {m} minutes for {m} marks is 1 minute per mark, so {q} marks get {q} minutes.","params":{"m":[40,80],"q":[2,12]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"An evaluate answer lists four good points for school uniform and stops. What is missing?","choices":["The other side and a final judgement","More detail on the four points","A diagram"],"answer":0,"hint":"Evaluate weighs both sides. Did it?","explain":"It needs points against and a judgement with a reason. More detail on one side still never evaluates."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Why mark your past paper answers with the mark scheme?","choices":["It shows exactly which points earn marks and which you missed","It is the same as reading the textbook","It tells you your final grade"],"answer":0,"hint":"What does a mark scheme list?","explain":"The scheme lists the points examiners reward, so you can see which ones your answer missed."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"A question is worth {m} marks. How many separate points does it usually need?","choices":["About {m}, one per mark","Just 1, however long","As many as you can fit"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the number in brackets.","explain":"Points-based questions often give one mark per point, so {m} marks usually need about {m} clear points.","params":{"m":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Six questions share 40 minutes. Q1 is 2 marks and Q6 is 12 marks. How should you split the time?","choices":["About 2 minutes on Q1 and 12 on Q6","The same time on each","Most time on Q1 because it comes first"],"answer":0,"hint":"Should time follow the marks or the order?","explain":"Time should follow the marks: about a minute a mark, so 2 minutes for Q1 and 12 for Q6."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Before writing, what is the first thing to do with a question?","choices":["Find the command word and the marks","Start writing everything you know","Skip to the hardest question"],"answer":0,"hint":"What tells you the kind of answer and how long to spend?","explain":"The command word says what kind of answer earns marks, and the marks say how much and how long."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"A question says: Describe the pattern in the graph. Which answer does what it asks?","choices":["Sales rose each year, fastest in 2021","Sales rose because prices fell","Electric cars are better than petrol cars"],"answer":0,"hint":"Describe asks what happens, not why or which is better.","explain":"Describe means say what the pattern is. Giving reasons is explain, and choosing a winner is evaluate."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A paper lasts {=3*m} minutes and has {=2*m} marks. About how many minutes should a {=2*h}-mark question get?","answer":"3*h","hint":"First find the minutes per mark: time divided by marks.","explain":"{=3*m} ÷ {=2*m} = 1.5 minutes per mark, so {=2*h} marks get 1.5 × {=2*h} = {=3*h} minutes.","params":{"m":[30,50],"h":[1,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Explain why ice melts faster in a warm room. [2 marks] Which answer earns both marks?","choices":["It melts faster because the air is warmer, so heat moves into the ice faster","It melts faster in a warm room","Ice is frozen water and it melts"],"answer":0,"hint":"Explain needs a reason. Look for because and so.","explain":"The first answer gives a reason and its effect. The others only say what happens or state a fact."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"A question is worth {m} marks. You have spent {=3*m} minutes on it and you are stuck. What is the best move?","choices":["Flag it, move on and come back at the end","Keep going until you get it","Leave it blank for good"],"answer":0,"hint":"How much time is it worth? What else could those minutes earn?","explain":"At about a minute a mark, {m} marks are worth about {m} minutes, and {=3*m} is far more. Move on and come back.","params":{"m":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Compare the two cities. Which sentence is a comparison?","choices":["City A is larger, whereas City B is older","City A is large and busy","I would rather live in City B"],"answer":0,"hint":"A comparison must talk about both things.","explain":"Compare needs both: whereas links a point about A to a point about B. A preference is not a comparison."}],"explain":{"kid":["Every exam question has a command word, such as describe, explain, compare or evaluate. It tells you the kind of answer that gets marks. A great answer to the wrong command word can still score low.","Describe means say what it is or what happens. Explain means give reasons, so use because and so. Compare means talk about both things and how they are alike or different. Evaluate means weigh up both sides, then make a judgement and say why.","Look at the marks in brackets. They tell you how many points to make and how long to spend. Work out the minutes per mark: time divided by marks. On many papers it is about a minute a mark. If you are stuck, flag the question, move on and come back.","Mark schemes show what examiners give marks for. Do past papers, then mark them with the scheme. You will see which points you missed, and why a long answer with no reasons can score zero."],"adult":["This topic covers how to read a question before answering it. Exam boards publish command words with set meanings. AQA, for example, defines explain as giving reasons, compare as setting out likenesses or differences rather than writing about only one thing, and evaluate as weighing evidence for and against to reach a judgement. The marks shown set how much is needed and how long to spend.","The lab works on five skills. Learners match four answers on one topic to the command word each one obeys. They upgrade a describe answer into an explain answer, and see that extra detail with no reason earns nothing. They share 45 minutes across six questions by marks, starting from an even split. They weigh points for and against before choosing a justified judgement, and mark three answers against a mark scheme.","The most common mistake is answering the topic and not the command word: describing when asked to explain, or writing about one thing when asked to compare. Another is ending an evaluate answer with no judgement, or a judgement with no reason. Learners also give every question the same time, or spend far too long on a low-mark question they are stuck on.","At home, pick a past paper question and ask your learner to circle the command word and say what it wants before writing. Then mark the answer together with the published mark scheme."]},"familyTip":"Before your teen answers a past paper question, ask them to circle the command word and say what it wants, then work out the minutes from the marks. Afterwards, mark it together with the published mark scheme.","keyIdea":"The command word tells you what kind of answer earns marks: describe says what happens, explain gives reasons, compare covers both things, and evaluate weighs both sides and then judges. The marks in brackets tell you how much to write and how long to spend, about a minute a mark on many papers. Mark schemes and past papers show exactly what earns each mark.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which answer does the command word want? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Describe: what happens","Explain: why it happens"],"items":[["The graph rises, then levels off",0],["Sales fell in 2020",0],["The leaf is broad and flat",0],["It rose because demand grew",1],["The leaf is broad so it catches more light",1],["It melted since the room was warm",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagexam","caption":"Flagship lab: match answers to command words, upgrade an answer to earn its marks, plan minutes by marks, weigh then judge, and mark like an examiner.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_8uFvTbk7EA":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"In the 2025 maths trial, how did students with an answer-giving AI do in the later exam without AI?","choices":["About 17% worse than students with no AI","About 48% better","Exactly the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"Practice and exam results were different.","explain":"They scored 48% higher in practice but 17% lower in the exam without AI. The 48% was practice, not the exam."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Why did the hint-giving tutor AI not cause the same drop?","choices":["Students still did the thinking themselves","It gave the answers faster","It was only used once"],"answer":0,"hint":"What did hints leave to the student?","explain":"Hints kept students working out each step, so they learned. Answers let them skip the thinking."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"An AI cites a study you cannot find anywhere. What should you do?","choices":["Do not use it until you find and check the real source","Use it: the AI must have read it","Cite the AI as the study"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can AI tools make up sources?","explain":"AI tools can invent references. Only cite sources you have found and checked yourself."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which is a real check of an AI's maths answer?","choices":["Put the answer back into the question","Ask the AI to say it again","Check it sounds confident"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one uses something outside the AI?","explain":"Substituting the answer back uses the question itself. Repeating or sounding sure proves nothing."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"You reword a paragraph an AI wrote and hand it in as your own. What is that?","choices":["Malpractice, even though you changed the words","Fine, because the words are now yours","Fine, if the facts are right"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does rewording change who did the work?","explain":"Copying or paraphrasing AI content as your own work is malpractice under the exam rules."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"An AI says {a}x + {b} = {=a*c+b} gives x = {=c+2}. What is the correct value of x?","answer":"c","hint":"Subtract {b}, then divide by {a}.","explain":"{=a*c+b} − {b} = {=a*c}, and {=a*c} ÷ {a} = {c}. The AI's answer gives {=a*(c+2)+b}, not {=a*c+b}.","params":{"a":[2,6],"b":[1,9],"c":[1,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"What is the best way to use an AI when revising for a test?","choices":["Ask it to quiz me, then check my answers","Ask for a summary and reread it","Ask for past paper answers to read"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one makes you recall?","explain":"Being quizzed makes you pull ideas from memory, which builds learning. Rereading answers feels easy but sticks less."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"An AI says {=10*p}% of {=10*n} is {=p*n+n}. Check it. What is the correct answer?","answer":"p*n","hint":"Find 10% first by dividing by 10.","explain":"10% of {=10*n} is {n}, so {=10*p}% is {p} × {n} = {=p*n}, not {=p*n+n}.","params":{"p":[1,8],"n":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Your teacher says no AI for this coursework task. What should you do?","choices":["Follow the rule: your teacher may limit AI use","Use it anyway if you acknowledge it","Use it only for the conclusion"],"answer":0,"hint":"Who decides how AI may be used on the task?","explain":"Schools may restrict AI use for a task. Acknowledging use does not make banned use acceptable."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which request to an AI builds your learning most?","choices":["Give me a hint for this question, not the answer","Write my answer for me","Rewrite my paragraph so I can submit it"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one still leaves the thinking to you?","explain":"A hint keeps you doing the thinking. The other two hand the work to the AI, so little is learned."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"An AI says the solution of {a}(x + {b}) = {=a*(c+b)} is x = {=c+1}. Check it by substitution. What is the correct value of x?","answer":"c","hint":"Divide both sides by {a} first, then subtract {b}.","explain":"Dividing by {a} gives x + {b} = {=c+b}, so x = {c}. The AI's {=c+1} gives {a} × {=c+1+b} = {=a*(c+1+b)}, which is not {=a*(c+b)}.","params":{"a":[2,5],"b":[1,6],"c":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"You ask an AI whether its answer is right and it says yes. What have you learned?","choices":["Very little: it can just agree with itself","That the answer is definitely right","That you can skip checking"],"answer":0,"hint":"Has anything outside the AI been used?","explain":"An AI can repeat its own mistake. A real check uses the question, a trusted source or a person."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"An AI says {=5*p}% of {=20*n} is {=p*n+2}. What is the correct answer?","answer":"p*n","hint":"Find 10% or 5% first, then build up.","explain":"{=5*p}% of {=20*n} is {=5*p} × {=20*n} ÷ 100 = {=p*n}, so the AI was wrong.","params":{"p":[1,6],"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"For history coursework, you used an AI to suggest sources. What must you do?","choices":["Acknowledge it: tool, date, saved prompts and replies, how you used it","Nothing: research help does not count","Just write AI in the bibliography"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does research help count as AI use?","explain":"Any AI use must be acknowledged with the tool name, date, a saved copy and how it was used. Just writing AI is not enough."}],"explain":{"kid":["Some help makes you learn, and some help just does the work. A hint, a quiz or an explanation that you then use yourself builds learning. Copying an answer or handing in work an AI wrote skips the thinking, so very little sticks.","A 2025 trial with about 1,000 high school maths students tested this. Students with an AI that gave answers did 48% better in practice. But in a later exam without AI, they did 17% worse than students who never had it. A tutor AI that gave hints, not answers, did not cause that drop.","AI tools can sound sure and still be wrong. They can even make up sources. So check the answer with something outside the AI. Put the answer back into the question, compare it with a textbook or mark scheme, or ask a teacher. Asking the AI if it is sure is not a check.","In exam coursework, you must say when you used AI. Give the tool's name, the date, a saved copy of your prompts and its replies, and how you used it. Your teacher may limit AI use. Copying or rewording AI text and calling it yours is malpractice, and marks only go to your own work."],"adult":["This topic helps older students use help, including AI chatbots, in a way that builds learning rather than replacing it. A large field trial (Bastani and colleagues, PNAS 2025) found that an answer-giving AI raised practice scores but lowered later exam scores without AI by 17%, while a hint-giving tutor version avoided that harm.","The lab works through five skills. Learners sort requests into help that builds learning and help that does the work. They find the faulty line in a confident AI solution and prove it wrong by substitution. They pick real checks over fake ones, read the trial's results, and build a coursework acknowledgement that meets the exam rules.","The most common mistake is treating fluent, well-set-out AI answers as correct, or asking the same AI whether it is sure. Another is believing that rewording AI text makes it your own work, or that research help does not need acknowledging. Under the UK exam boards' rules, both count as malpractice in coursework.","At home, when your teen uses an AI tool, ask what they asked it, how they checked the answer, and what they can now do without it. A good test is whether they can solve a similar problem on their own the next day."]},"familyTip":"When your teen uses an AI tool, ask three questions: what did you ask it, how did you check its answer, and could you now do a similar problem on your own?","keyIdea":"Good help leaves the thinking to you: hints, quizzes and explanations you then act on. AI tools can be fluent and wrong, so check their answers against something outside the AI, such as the question itself or a trusted source. In coursework, any AI use must be acknowledged, and copying or rewording AI text as your own is malpractice.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Coursework rules: drag each use of AI, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["OK if allowed and acknowledged","Malpractice"],"items":[["Using AI to find sources, then checking them",0],["Asking AI to explain a hard idea, then writing my own notes",0],["Using AI to quiz yourself",0],["Copying a paragraph from AI",1],["Rewording AI text as my own",1],["Using AI without saying so",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagai","caption":"Flagship lab: sort helpful from harmful requests, catch a confident AI's algebra slip, pick real checks, read a 2025 trial and build an honest acknowledgement.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_CmjNF5Pt2J":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Gran says, \"Touch your nose, then touch your toes.\" Which is right?","choices":["Nose, then toes","Toes, then nose","Toes only"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which part came after the word then?","explain":"Nose comes first and toes come second, because Gran said touch your nose, then touch your toes."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Mum says, \"Put the ball in the box and then come for lunch.\" Tom runs to lunch and leaves the ball. Is that right?","choices":["No, the ball goes in the box first","Yes, that is right","Yes, any order is fine"],"answer":0,"hint":"Tom did the last part he heard. What came before it?","explain":"Tom only did the last part. Mum asked for the ball in the box first, then lunch."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"How many jobs are in \"Get your coat and hang it on the hook\"?","choices":["Two jobs","One job","Three jobs"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count the things to do: get it, then hang it.","explain":"There are two jobs: get your coat, then hang it on the hook."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Gran says, \"Find your hat, then put on your boots.\" What do you do second?","choices":["Put on my boots","Find my hat","Take off my coat"],"answer":0,"hint":"Second means the job after the first one.","explain":"Find your hat is the first job and put on your boots is the second job."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Dad is talking. You want to remember his two jobs. What helps most?","choices":["Listen to the end, then say it back","Start before he finishes","Look at the TV"],"answer":0,"hint":"How can you be sure you have both parts?","explain":"Listening to the end and then saying the two jobs back helps you hold both parts in your head."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Gran says, \"Put your {n} blocks in the box, then wash your hands.\" What do you do first?","choices":["Put the blocks in the box","Wash my hands","Build a tower"],"answer":0,"hint":"The first job is the one Gran said first.","explain":"Putting the blocks away is the first job. Washing your hands comes next, after the blocks.","params":{"n":[3,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Mum says two jobs. Ana does the first one, then stops. There are {n} more minutes till bath time. What should Ana do now?","choices":["Do the second job","Go and play","Do the first job again"],"answer":0,"hint":"Mum said two jobs. How many has Ana done?","explain":"Ana has done one of the two jobs. She should do the second job now, and ask again if she has forgotten it.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"You did not hear the end of what Gran said. What can you say?","choices":["\"Can you say it again, please?\"","\"I did it\"","\"No\""],"answer":0,"hint":"Use kind words to ask Gran for help.","explain":"Asking Gran to say it again is the right thing to do, so you can hear both parts before you start."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Gran says, \"First clap, then jump.\" What do you do first?","choices":["Clap","Jump","Sit down"],"answer":0,"hint":"Listen for the word first. What comes right after it?","explain":"Gran said clap first. The word first tells you which job to do now, and then tells you the next job."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Mum says, \"Put your cup in the sink, then get your coat.\" Ben has {n} toys out, but he does not touch them. What does he do first?","choices":["Put his cup in the sink","Get his coat","Play with his toys"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which job did Mum say first?","explain":"Mum said the cup first, so Ben puts his cup in the sink and then gets his coat. The toys were not part of the job.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Dad says two jobs. You forget the second one. What do you do?","choices":["Ask, \"Can you say it again, please?\"","Make up a job","Do nothing"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is it OK to ask a grown-up to help you remember?","explain":"Asking to hear it again is a good thing to do. Guessing can lead to the wrong job, and doing nothing leaves the job undone."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Gran says, \"Get your shoes and bring them to the door.\" Mia gets her shoes and sits down with them. She has {n} minutes left before the walk. What did she forget?","choices":["To bring them to the door","To get her shoes","Nothing"],"answer":0,"hint":"Gran said two jobs. Mia did one. Which one is left?","explain":"Getting the shoes was part one. Bringing them to the door was part two, and Mia has not done it yet.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Teacher says, \"Wash your hands, then sit at the table.\" How can you say it back?","choices":["Wash hands, then sit down","Sit down, then wash hands","Wash hands, then go outside"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say the two jobs in the same order you heard them.","explain":"Saying it back in the same order, wash hands then sit down, helps you remember both parts and shows you heard them."}],"explain":{"kid":["Gran says, \"Get your shoes.\" That is one job. You get your shoes.","Gran says, \"Get your shoes and bring them to the door.\" That is two jobs!","Listen to the end. Do the first job first. Then do the next job.","Did you forget? That is OK. Ask, \"Can you say it again, please?\" Then say it back: shoes, then door."],"adult":["This topic, for ages 3 to 4, is about carrying out an instruction with two parts, such as \"Put your cup in the sink and then fetch your coat.\" It asks a young child to listen to the whole sentence, hold both parts in mind while they act (early working memory), and keep the order. It grows out of being able to pay attention to one thing, and it is the base for following classroom routines later.","In the lab, your child taps one thing Gran asks for, then two things in a first-then order, then gets an object and takes it to a place (shoes to the door, cup to the sink). Next the words hide and they work from memory, with a \"Say it again, please\" button that is praised, not punished. Last, they put picture cards in order to say the steps back.","Common mistakes: doing only the last part heard (the most recent words are easiest to recall), doing the parts in the wrong order, and doing the first part and then drifting off. Adults often add to the load by giving three or four steps at once, or by talking while the child is busy. Get their attention first, say it once, slowly, and ask them to tell you the two jobs before they start.","Use everyday jobs: \"Put your socks in the basket, then come for your story.\" Point or show as you say each part, and praise your child for asking when they forget."]},"familyTip":"Play \"Gran says\" at home: give a two-part job such as \"Touch the door, then jump\" and let your child do it, then swap so they give you a two-part job. Make a mistake on purpose (do it in the wrong order) and let them catch you.","keyIdea":"A two-step instruction asks for two things in order, such as \"Get your shoes and bring them to the door.\" To follow it you listen to the end, keep both parts in your head, do the first part first and then the second. If you forget a part, it is fine to ask, \"Can you say it again, please?\" Saying the steps back helps you remember them.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"One job or two jobs? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["One job","Two jobs"],"items":[["touch the ball",0],["get your coat",0],["sit down",0],["get your shoes and bring them to the door",1],["wash your hands, then sit down",1],["put your cup in the sink, then get your coat",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagtwo","caption":"Flagship lab: do one thing Gran asks, then two things in order, get it and bring it, remember both parts when the words hide, and say the steps back.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_oVa6O3-Vck":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What does it mean to be well calibrated?","choices":["Your answers rated 70% sure are right about 70% of the time","You are always 100% sure","You never make mistakes"],"answer":0,"hint":"Calibration compares how sure you say you are with how often you are right.","explain":"Calibration means your confidence matches your hit rate at each level, so 70% sure answers are right about 70% of the time."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"Zara said \"80% sure\" on 20 answers and got {n} right. What percentage did she get right?","answer":"n*5","hint":"Each answer out of 20 is worth 5%.","explain":"{n} out of 20 is {n} × 5 = {=n*5}%. Compare that with the 80% she claimed.","params":{"n":[8,15]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"You feel 100% sure that 0.3 × 0.2 = 0.6. What should you do?","choices":["Check another way: 3 × 2 = 6 with two decimal places gives 0.06","Move on, because certain means right","Rub it out and leave it blank"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can a fast answer feel certain and still be wrong?","explain":"Check it another way. 3 × 2 = 6, and there are two decimal places, so the answer is 0.06, not 0.6. Certainty is not proof."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which habit improves calibration over time?","choices":["Rating confidence before checking, then comparing with the results","Only checking answers you feel unsure about","Never thinking about how sure you are"],"answer":0,"hint":"Forecasters get better because they get feedback on their forecasts.","explain":"Rating confidence first and then comparing with the real results gives the feedback that makes calibration better."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Ella's chart shows: said 90%, right 6 of 10; said 50%, right 5 of 10. Where is she overconfident?","choices":["At 90%","At 50%","Nowhere"],"answer":0,"hint":"Compare each claim with the rate: 6 of 10 is 60%, 5 of 10 is 50%.","explain":"At 90% she was right only 60% of the time, so she is overconfident there. At 50% her rate matched her claim."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"During homework you are about 50% sure of a date in History. Your textbook is on the desk. What is the best move?","choices":["Look it up in the textbook","Write the guess and move on","Skip the question"],"answer":0,"hint":"Doubt plus a source to hand. What turns a guess into knowledge?","explain":"Look it up. Checking takes a minute and the doubt you noticed tells you exactly where to check."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"A friend says, \"I got one 'certain' answer wrong, so my confidence is useless.\" What is the best reply?","choices":["Look at the rate over many answers, not one result","Yes, stop rating confidence","Confidence is always right in the end"],"answer":0,"hint":"Even a perfectly calibrated 90% means about 1 in 10 is wrong.","explain":"One wrong answer is not a verdict. Calibration is about the rate over many answers, and even 90% sure means about 1 in 10 will be wrong."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Over {n} weeks, Sam's \"certain\" answers are right 99% of the time. What does this suggest?","choices":["His certainty is well calibrated on this material","He is overconfident","He should stop checking anything"],"answer":0,"hint":"Compare the claim (certain, about 100%) with the result.","explain":"Right 99% of the time on certain answers over {n} weeks matches his claim, so he is well calibrated here. Quick checks on fast answers still help.","params":{"n":[3,8]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Kai said \"90% sure\" on 20 answers and got {n} right. Which describes Kai?","choices":["Overconfident","Underconfident","Well calibrated"],"answer":0,"hint":"Work out right ÷ 20 as a percentage and compare it with 90%.","explain":"{n} out of 20 is {=n*5}%, which is below the 90% Kai claimed, so Kai was overconfident.","params":{"n":[10,15]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A calibrated forecaster says \"{p}0% chance of rain\" on 30 different days. On about how many of those days should it rain?","answer":"p*3","hint":"Calibrated means the rate of rain matches the forecast: {p}0% of 30.","explain":"{p}0% of 30 days is {=p*3} days, so a calibrated forecaster sees rain on about {=p*3} of them.","params":{"p":[1,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which kind of error is hardest to catch on your own?","choices":["Sure and wrong","Unsure and wrong","Unsure and right"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which answers do you usually not bother to check?","explain":"Sure-and-wrong errors are hardest to catch, because people re-check what feels shaky and skip what feels certain."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Mo marked {n} answers \"just guessing (50%)\" and all of them were right. Which is the best conclusion?","choices":["Mo may be underconfident on this topic","Guessing always works","Mo is overconfident"],"answer":0,"hint":"Was Mo right more or less often than his confidence said?","explain":"Mo said 50% but was right on all {n}, more often than he claimed, which points to underconfidence. A short run can be luck, so he should check over more answers.","params":{"n":[4,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"In a test with no penalty for wrong answers, you are 40% sure of an answer. What is the best move?","choices":["Write your best answer, flag it and come back if there is time","Leave it blank","Spend 15 minutes on it now"],"answer":0,"hint":"A blank always scores zero. What keeps a chance of marks and protects your time?","explain":"Write your best answer and flag it. A blank cannot score, and getting stuck costs time you need for other questions."}],"explain":{"kid":["Feeling sure and being right are two different things. You can feel 90% sure and still be wrong a lot. The only way to know is to check your sure answers against the real ones.","Being calibrated means your sureness matches your results. If you say \"70% sure\" on ten answers, about seven should be right. Weather forecasters do this well, because they get feedback every day.","Sort your answers into four boxes: sure and right, sure and wrong, unsure and right, unsure and wrong. Sure and wrong is the risky box. You don't check what you feel sure of, so those slips get through.","Doubt is useful. When you feel unsure, check it, ask, or look it up. In a test with no penalty for wrong answers, write your best answer and flag it. When you feel certain about a fast answer, check it another way."],"adult":["This topic, for ages 14 to 16, is about judging how sure to be and then acting on that judgement. A learner is well calibrated when the share of answers they get right at each level of confidence matches that level. In studies with general-knowledge questions, people tend to be overconfident on hard items. On easy items they can be underconfident. This is called the hard-easy effect.","Calibration gets better with frequent, clear feedback. Weather forecasters are a classic example of good calibration, and learners improve the same way: rate your confidence before you see the answer, then compare. Errors made with high confidence need special care. Students who only re-check the answers that felt shaky will miss them. Yet once they are corrected, these errors are often remembered well. This is the hypercorrection effect.","Common mistakes: treating a feeling of certainty as proof, and reading one lucky or unlucky result as a verdict instead of looking at the rate over many answers. Learners also mix up over- and underconfidence, and leave answers blank in tests that do not take marks off for wrong answers. Some freeze on a doubtful question instead of flagging it and moving on.","In the lab, learners reveal a classmate's \"90% sure\" answers and mark his true hit rate. They set rainy days to make a forecaster calibrated and plot their own calibration chart. Then they sort answers into the four confidence boxes, checking each one, and choose the best action for doubt in four situations."]},"familyTip":"Before checking homework answers, ask your teenager to write a confidence next to each one (50, 70, 90 or 100%). After marking, count how many of the 90% answers were right. Talk about any sure-and-wrong ones first.","keyIdea":"How sure you feel is a claim you can test. You are calibrated when your \"70% sure\" answers are right about 70% of the time. Most people are overconfident on hard questions, and the most dangerous errors are the ones you feel sure of, because you never check them. Use doubt as a signal: check, ask, look it up or flag it, rather than guessing or freezing.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Over, under or calibrated? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Overconfident","Calibrated","Underconfident"],"items":[["said 90%, right 6 of 10",0],["said 100%, right 7 of 10",0],["said 70%, right 7 of 10",1],["said 50%, right 5 of 10",1],["said 50%, right 9 of 10",2],["said 60%, right 10 of 10",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagcalib","caption":"Flagship lab: check a 90%-sure classmate, calibrate a forecaster, chart your own confidence and decide what to do with doubt.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_55gVrNZ8ro":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"When do you start copying?","choices":["When the show is done","Before it starts","Halfway, so I am fast"],"answer":0,"hint":"You need to see all of it before you can copy all of it.","explain":"Watch to the end first. Then you have seen every step to copy."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"A dog runs past while Gran shows you a song. Where do your eyes go?","choices":["On Gran","On the dog","Up at the sky"],"answer":0,"hint":"Who is showing you the song?","explain":"Keep your eyes on Gran. If you watch the dog, you miss the moves."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Owl waves, then claps. Which is the copy?","choices":["Wave, then clap","Clap, then wave","Wave, wave, wave"],"answer":0,"hint":"Same moves, in the same order as Owl.","explain":"Owl waved first and clapped second, so you wave, then clap."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Tidying up has {n} steps. It is hard to copy. What helps?","choices":["Watch again, slowly","Skip some steps","Go fast"],"answer":0,"hint":"Long jobs are easier to see when they go slowly.","explain":"Watching the {n} steps again, slowly, helps you see each one.","params":{"n":[4,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Your baby brother watches you stack {n} blocks. What might he do?","choices":["Copy you","Fly away","Read a book"],"answer":0,"hint":"Little ones learn by watching big ones.","explain":"He saw you stack {n} blocks, so he may try to copy you.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"You copied a dance, but one move was wrong. What now?","choices":["Watch that bit again","Say I am bad at it","Never dance again"],"answer":0,"hint":"A wrong move just means you need to see it once more.","explain":"Watch that bit again and try it. Getting a step wrong is part of learning."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Dad wipes the table. What do you watch?","choices":["Dad's hands and the cloth","Dad's shoes","The ceiling"],"answer":0,"hint":"Where is the job happening?","explain":"The job is in Dad's hands and the cloth, so that is where to look."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Mum shows you how to put on a sock. What do you do first?","choices":["Watch Mum's hands","Grab the sock right away","Look out of the window"],"answer":0,"hint":"To copy, you need to see how it is done.","explain":"Watch Mum's hands first. Then you know what to do when it is your turn."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Owl shows {n} claps, then a stomp. What is the copy?","choices":["{n} claps, then a stomp","A stomp, then {n} claps","Just a stomp"],"answer":0,"hint":"A copy has the same moves in the same order.","explain":"Owl did {n} claps first, then a stomp, so the copy is {n} claps, then a stomp.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"You are copying Dad, but you miss a step. What can you say?","choices":["Show me again, slowly","I can't do it","Nothing, just stop"],"answer":0,"hint":"Missing a step is OK. How can you see it again?","explain":"Asking to see it again, slowly, lets you catch the step you missed."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Owl stacks {n} cups. You copy. How many cups go on your stack?","choices":["The same as Owl","One more than Owl","Just one"],"answer":0,"hint":"Copying means making it the same as Owl's.","explain":"A copy matches Owl, so your stack has {n} cups too, in the same places.","params":{"n":[2,5]}}],"explain":{"kid":["You can learn by watching. Gran stirs the cake mix. You watch her hands. Then you have a go. That is copying.","Watch it all first. Do not grab the spoon too soon. When Gran is done, it is your turn.","Keep your eyes on the one who shows you. If you look at the dog, you miss it. Do the same steps in the same order: clap, then stomp, then wave.","Missed a bit? That is OK. Say, \"Show me again, slowly.\" Little ones watch you too. They copy what you do."],"adult":["Toddlers learn a great deal by imitation. From around their first birthday, children copy actions they have seen, and by two and three they can watch a short sequence, such as an action song, stacking cups or wiping a table, and then reproduce it. This topic is about doing that on purpose: watching closely, waiting until the demonstration ends, and copying the steps in order.","The lab gives each part its own action. Children watch Owl's three-move dance and copy it, copy a cup stack by looking at Owl's stack, choose to watch Owl rather than a butterfly, ask to watch the five hand-washing steps again slowly, and then teach Chick a dance and see Chick copy them.","Common mistakes to watch for: starting before the demonstration has finished, copying only one part, swapping the order, and looking away at something more exciting. Another slip is giving up after missing a step rather than asking to see it again. Adults make it harder by showing too fast or by taking over and doing it for the child.","To help, show slowly with few words, name each step as you do it, and pause so your child can have a go. Children copy everything they see, including how we react to mistakes, so show calm second tries."]},"familyTip":"Pick a small job, such as wiping the table or stacking three cups. Say, \"Watch my hands,\" and do it slowly, naming each step. Then say, \"Your turn.\" If a step is missed, show just that part again rather than doing it for them.","keyIdea":"You can learn a new thing by watching someone do it and then copying them. Keep your eyes on the one who shows you, watch all the way to the end, then do the same steps in the same order. If you miss a bit, ask to see it again, slowly.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"What helps you copy? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["👀 Helps me copy","🙈 Makes it hard"],"items":[["eyes on the helper",0],["watch to the end",0],["ask to see it again",0],["go slowly",0],["look at the TV",1],["grab it right away",1],["shut my eyes",1],["run off",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagcopy","caption":"Flagship lab: watch Owl's dance and copy it, copy a cup stack, keep eyes on Owl, ask to see hand-washing again, then teach Chick.","opts":{"step":0}}}});
